Ladybird now paints a non-repeating linear gradient whose stops are
outside 0%-100% by running its gradient line from its first stop to its
last, clips a conic gradient’s stops to 0 and 1 with edge colors
interpolated in its interpolation space, and collapses a radial
gradient’s negative stops to 0 the same way, scaling its ending shape
for stops past 1. Blink does the same in CSSGradientValue::AddStops():
It moves the endpoints of a linear gradient and the angles of a conic
one by AdjustedGradientDomainForOffsetRange(), and for a radial one runs
ClampNegativeOffsets() and then AdjustGradientRadiiForOffsetRange().
WebKit’s normalizeStopsAndEndpointsOutsideRange() (StyleGradient.cpp)
rescales a linear gradient’s stops and endpoints, and for radial and
conic gradients collapses negative stops to 0 with a color from
interpolateColors() in the gradient’s interpolation method. Gecko’s
nsCSSGradientRenderer adjusts a linear gradient’s line and runs
ClampColorStops(), and moves a radial gradient’s negative stops to 0
with the interpolated color. In Chrome 158, Firefox 151 and WebKit 26.5
(engine-compare), the issue’s cases, an angled gradient, a hard stop,
oklab and hsl interpolation and a repeating gradient all render as
Ladybird now does, within one level per channel.
